{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for journal-floor draft 3 at exact 45625474da63a4eb572ea15958097121137902a1, tree 4fb38ccabfbc77cfca4c87048651b905ae534ce4. Primary 93b7dca0 was read first in full (603 lines). This is the complete design review, with all remaining findings together.\n\n1. [P2] Specify how completed request state survives archiving its sending entry. Sections 4.1 item 3 and 6.7 keep pending request origins so REQUEST_ROWS still joins entry, but that is not the full checkpoint state. SqliteStore.all loads every request through that inner join (sqlite.ts:118,376), including resolved/diagnosed requests. MemoryState.putRequest retains those completed rows in #requests (state.ts:543–547), and all() exports every one at :578; stateDigest hashes that whole snapshot at :217–219. Consider an acknowledged transition send at 100, completed at 150, with no live item opening or other retained dependency requiring its origin, followed by floor 500. Deleting entry 100 removes its completed OwnRequest from the SQLite snapshot, whereas a replay that folds the journal and performs only the stated floor effects retains it. The note adds accepted-key and inbox summaries but supplies neither a completed-request summary nor a shared disposition for these rows. If the broad “each own position ... current state names” in 4.1 item 5 instead means all request origins remain kept forever, say so explicitly: that changes retained entry growth and can exhaust floor-kept after 4,096 completed sends. Define one treatment for both stores and replay, with a completed/acknowledged request below two floors, restart, request lookup and checkpoint/replay equality in the witness. This is a missing design rule, not a claim about an implemented floor.\n\n2. [P2] Give v2 retained files stable bounded paths instead of inheriting v1's relocation rule. Section 2.2 says retained files are “as in v1”, while 2.3 rules 4 and 7 only add the new pass's files and rewrite their tree paths. The v1 layout uses the complete retained-name set to choose physical paths (journal-files.ts:26–48; journal-segments.ts:65–88). DIRECTORY_NAMES is 4,096 (:8), and crossing that threshold moves every old leaf into digest-prefix directories. In v2, eight passes can each add 512 distinct retained keys in one kind/domain; one new key in the next pass moves all 4,096 old paths if v1's canonical layout is retained. A partial new-key-only plan instead disagrees with that layout's reader, and keeping the old flat directory violates the stated bound on tree work. Specify v2's stable retained fan-out from the first file, or an explicit bounded relocation/reader protocol and its charged work. Extend the tree-work witness across this retained-key boundary, not only 10,000 passes that add no new retained keys. This is the unresolved part of prior finding 5.\n\n3. [P3] Bound the top numeric directory as well as its descendants. Section 2.2:90–93 takes a as the first six of twelve decimal digits and b as the next three, then says no passes/segments directory lists more than 1,000 names. The leaf and b levels meet that bound, but the top passes/ or segments/ directory can contain a million a names. Reaching first positions 000000000000, 000001000000, ... 001000000000 produces 1,001 top-level names within the stated twelve-digit range. Split all numeric levels into groups of at most three digits, or state and charge a different complete bound and maximum scope position. This is a large cumulative-scope case requiring a higher offline-reader allowance, not an observed Room A failure.\n\nThe other five findings of 782f1656 are resolved in the proposal. The kept set is now a function of state plus the journaled FloorInput.keep list; transport acknowledgements and signer readiness remain writer checks, and cannot independently change replay's set. Size proofs retain positions and only strictly earlier rows may prove a binding. V1 conversion explicitly seeds the first v2 pass at 0 from a confirmed receipt, with pending v1 work handled first; subsequent work continues after through. The capacity witness now uses scopeEntries 2,000, margin 200 and minimum 500, with unchanged judge limits and a stated special admission at the bound. Claim-discovery entries are kept and rebuilt after restart, with no authority inferred merely from archive coverage. The forecast tense is corrected. The exact current recorded-repository decision 7d947a6b was checked; no dedicated repository or destination override was assumed.\n\nVerification: all changes from D2 eec4ec0 are confined to this note, and all 2,569 other tracked paths remain identical to the declared source baseline 7a148325. Exact source was preserved for 43 cited/related files, and the newly relevant acknowledgements, signer metadata, strict-before proof scan, claim discovery, membership access, request snapshots and archive layout/budget rows were checked. journal-files.ts, journal-segments.ts and journal-path.ts also remain byte-exact on actual published Main 9c6d344c. Native Source is current/effective at exact candidate, directly rests on bb6edf4a, and supplies the matching live f9fcee34 commitment; native prepare assigns that implementation.\n\nThe counterexamples are static protocol/schema and path arithmetic, with exact source citations. No v2/floor implementation was executed; no runtime floor failure, native admission, provider or replay execution is claimed. Documentation-only whitespace check passed (exit 0); no project tests, gate, package installation or tool probes were run. Trees: D2 70dba9a9f85cd96accf948379f5b71f7a0a49501; D3 4fb38ccabfbc77cfca4c87048651b905ae534ce4; baseline 4b5a2fa12d92c083ddcdd1ffd61fbd15d090969b. Checker source-anchor guesses were corrected to actual lines before recording evidence; a missing guessed filename was not treated as a source defect. No shared source/ref changes, merge, push, custody, hosting or deployment action. Return a fresh exact-head design review after